    "body": "> What is the select's button? Isn't it the select element itself, which has the appearance: menulist-button style in the the drop-down box state?\r\n\r\nYes, it is the select element itself. Instead of saying \"the text rendered in the select's button\", I could say \"the selected option text\", but maybe that is also less clear. I could also just remove the word \"button\" and keep the sentence otherwise the same. What do you think?\r\n\r\n> I'm trying to understand if this is different from \"the width necessary to render the widest option element in the element's list of options (including its indent, if any)\" or if maybe something could be added to the parenthetical? If it's about the little arrow, maybe the parenthetical could be \"including its indent and surrounding UI, if any\"?\r\n\r\nSorry the reasoning behind this wasn't clear - this is due to [a bug I found](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/386255692) where the select element was made wide enough to accomodate all of the options, but not the text for a multi-select where it says \"X selected\", so the \"X selected\" text would get chopped off if all the options had shorter text than \"X selected\".\r\n\r\nWhat If I added \"such as '3 selected'\" to the end of the last sentence as an example?",
